---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const aboutJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ProfilePage',
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: 'Matt Hurley',
    description: 'Software engineer and school leader focused on practical systems and privacy-first architecture.',
    url: 'https://matthurley.dev/about'
  }
};
---

<Layout
  title="About | matthurley.dev"
  description="Matt Hurley builds matthurley.dev around a dual seat: senior school leadership by day, practical software engineering by design."
  jsonLd={aboutJsonLd}
>
  <div class="container page-shell">
    <section class="page-hero hero-split reveal">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1>The person behind matthurley.dev.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          I work in senior leadership at a Catholic secondary school in South East Queensland while building
          software that solves genuine operational problems — for that school, and for people like the client
          behind Radium PCs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-panel">
        <div class="hero-panel-head">
          <span>profile.json</span>
          <span>Matt Hurley</span>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-stat-list">
          <div class="hero-stat">
            <span class="stat-value">11+ yrs</span>
            <span class="stat-label">In education, from classroom teaching through to senior leadership</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-stat">
            <span class="stat-value">1</span>
            <span class="stat-label">Person accountable for every line shipped — no hand-off, no diffusion of ownership</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-stat">
            <span class="stat-value">QLD, AU</span>
            <span class="stat-label">Based in South East Queensland, working with clients remotely</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="surface reveal">
      <h2>Why the dual seat matters</h2>
      <p>
        Most software gets designed by someone who has never had to operate it under pressure. I don't have that
        problem. Across more than a decade in education — classroom teaching through pastoral and assistant
        principal roles — the job has been the same: keep systems clear, reliable, and sustainable enough that
        people actually trust them when it counts.
      </p>
      <p class="muted mb-0">
        That's the same standard I hold engineering work to: practical architecture, explicit constraints, and
        measurable utility — not a demo that only survives the first walkthrough.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="surface reveal">
      <h2>Background</h2>
      <div class="highlight-grid">
        <article class="topic-card">
          <h3>Education Leadership</h3>
          <p>Pastoral and assistant principal responsibilities across wellbeing, engagement, and operational systems — the direct source of STARS.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="topic-card">
          <h3>Military Service</h3>
          <p>Service in the Australian Army built the foundation this all sits on: discipline, responsibility, and clear decision-making under pressure.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="topic-card">
          <h3>Technical Development</h3>
          <p>Independent and client software spanning educational platforms, hardware control, and privacy-preserving desktop tools.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="topic-card">
          <h3>Ongoing Learning</h3>
          <p>Postgraduate leadership studies alongside continued, deliberate practice in cybersecurity and systems design.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="surface reveal">
      <h2>How I work with clients</h2>
      <div class="value-grid">
        <article class="value-card">
          <span class="value-icon">01</span>
          <h3>People First</h3>
          <p>Technology should reduce burden and improve outcomes for real users, not introduce complexity for its own sake.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="value-card">
          <span class="value-icon">02</span>
          <h3>Privacy by Default</h3>
          <p>Data protection is built into architecture from the start, not retrofitted after something goes wrong.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="value-card">
          <span class="value-icon">03</span>
          <h3>Practical Systems</h3>
          <p>Good solutions account for staffing realities, governance requirements, and long-term maintainability.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="value-card">
          <span class="value-icon">04</span>
          <h3>Continuous Improvement</h3>
          <p>Refinement is ongoing: observe results, learn from constraints, and iterate deliberately — not a one-shot ship.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="support-banner reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Get In Touch</p>
      <h2 class="mb-3">Based in South East Queensland, working wherever the project needs</h2>
      <p class="muted">If the background above sounds like the right judgment for your project, let's talk about it.</p>
      <div class="button-row mt-3">
        <a href="/contact" class="button primary">Start a project</a>
        <a href="/projects" class="button secondary">See the work</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
